mode: "100644"
text: "# Formats — caption archetypes\n\nA format gives a caption a proven shape. Pick the one that fits the asset, the point, and the\ngoal — then write it in the brand's voice. Don't default to the same format every time; variety\nis what keeps a feed from feeling like a template.\n\nFor each: when to use it, its shape, and a compressed example (write the real thing in voice).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Story / micro-narrative\n**Use when:** you have a real moment, lesson, or before→after. Highest trust and shareability.\n**Shape:** drop into the moment → tension → turn → lesson/payoff.\n**Example skeleton:** \"Lost our biggest client on a Tuesday. Here's what it taught me about ___.\"\n**Goal fit:** awareness, community, conversion (via trust).\n\n## 2. Value / how-to\n**Use when:** you can teach something useful in a few lines. The most \"save-able\" format.\n**Shape:** promise the outcome → deliver 3–5 tight, concrete steps/points → CTA to save.\n**Example skeleton:** \"How to ___ in 4 steps: 1) … 2) … 3) … 4) … Save this for next time.\"\n**Goal fit:** saves, authority.\n\n## 3. Hot take / contrarian\n**Use when:** the brand has a real point of view (from brand-profile). Drives comments.\n**Shape:** state the unpopular position plainly → back it in 2–3 lines → invite disagreement.\n**Example skeleton:** \"Posting daily is overrated. Here's what actually grew our account → ___.\"\n**Goal fit:** comments, reach. Stay on-brand; never manufacture outrage.\n\n## 4. Question / fill-in-the-blank\n**Use when:** the goal is comments and the topic invites opinion or identity.\n**Shape:** quick setup → one sharp, easy-to-answer question.\n**Example skeleton:** \"Which one are you — ___ or ___? (No wrong answers.)\"\n**Goal fit:** comments, community. Keep the question low-effort to answer.\n\n## 5. Behind-the-scenes\n**Use when:** humanizing the brand, showing process, building familiarity.\n**Shape:** \"here's the part you don't usually see\" → the real detail → why it matters.\n**Goal fit:** community, trust. Especially strong for personal and local brands.\n\n## 6. Social proof / testimonial\n**Use when:** you have a real result, review, or customer story.\n**Shape:** the result up front (number/quote) → brief context → soft CTA.\n**Example skeleton:** \"'___' — said a customer who ___. Here's what we did → ___.\"\n**Goal fit:** conversion, trust. Use only verifiable proof per the brand profile.\n\n## 7. Announcement / launch\n**Use when:** something is genuinely new. Lead with the benefit, not the fanfare.\n**Shape:** the thing + why it matters to *them* → one clear next step.\n**Avoid:** \"We're thrilled to announce…\" (banned in most voice profiles). Lead with the value.\n**Goal fit:** awareness, conversion.\n\n## 8. Relatable / \"it's just me?\"\n**Use when:** a shared frustration or moment your audience will recognize.\n**Shape:** name the specific, slightly oddly-specific experience → land the feeling.\n**Example skeleton:** \"Opening 14 tabs to avoid starting the one task. Just me?\"\n**Goal fit:** reach, community. The specificity is what makes it travel.\n\n## 9. List / listicle\n**Use when:** multiple quick points; great for carousels and saves.\n**Shape:** promise → numbered/■ items, one line each → CTA to save.\n**Goal fit:** saves, authority.\n\n## 10. Carousel / swipe intro\n**Use when:** the asset is a carousel. The caption's job is to pull people into slide 1.\n**Shape:** hook that promises what the swipe delivers → \"→\" / \"keep reading\" only if on-voice →\nthe payoff lives in the slides; the caption sets it up.\n**Goal fit:** saves, dwell.\n\n---\n\n## Choosing a format\n\n- **Goal = saves** → value/how-to or list.\n- **Goal = comments** → hot take or question.\n- **Goal = reach** → story or relatable.\n- **Goal = conversion** → social proof or announcement (benefit-led).\n- **Goal = community** → behind-the-scenes or question.\n\nWhen unsure, the **story** and **value** formats are the safest high-performers across almost\nevery brand. Rotate formats over a content calendar so the feed stays varied.\n"
